{"schemaVersion": "docquery-n5.3-a1-answer-pilot-v1", "caseId": "mmlongbench-r3-0016", "sourceRowIndex": 16, "sourceDomain": "Tutorial/Workshop", "answerFormat": "Str", "referenceAnswer": "Elements of objects tend to be perceptually grouped together if they form a pattern that is regular, simple, and orderly.", "question": "How does this document define the law of good gestalt?", "mode": "HYBRID", "topK": 10, "http": {"ok": true, "httpStatus": 200, "requestId": "3204483c-43b8-4b2c-bf95-a0d8d7eaed67", "latencyMs": 5863.282, "finalAttemptLatencyMs": 5863.282, "attemptCount": 1, "attempts": [{"ok": true, "httpStatus": 200, "requestId": "3204483c-43b8-4b2c-bf95-a0d8d7eaed67", "latencyMs": 5863.282}]}, "response": {"queryExecutionId": "12fee8d3-ce71-44dc-b49e-44bea266af32", "status": "ANSWERED", "answer": "The law of good Gestalt states that elements of objects tend to be perceptually grouped together if they form a pattern that is regular, simple, and orderly. This law implies that as individuals perceive the world, they eliminate complexity and unfamiliarity so they can observe a reality in its most simplistic form, since eliminating extraneous stimuli helps the mind create meaning [E1][E31]. The law of good gestalt focuses on the idea of conciseness, which is what all of gestalt theory is based on [E2].", "requestedMode": "HYBRID", "executedMode": "HYBRID", "degraded": false, "degradationReason": null, "citations": [{"evidenceId": "E1", "documentKey": "mmlongbench-220685f26bdeb0798cef", "documentName": "0e94b4197b10096b1f4c699701570fbf.pdf", "headingPath": ["0e94b4197b10096b1f4c699701570fbf.pdf", "USSPACETRAVEL A TIMELINEOF MANNED NASA FLIGHTS"], "blockId": "9:b:000031", "text": "Law of good Gestalt. Elements of objects tend to be", "truncated": false, "pageNumber": 11}, {"evidenceId": "E31", "documentKey": "mmlongbench-220685f26bdeb0798cef", "documentName": "0e94b4197b10096b1f4c699701570fbf.pdf", "headingPath": ["0e94b4197b10096b1f4c699701570fbf.pdf", "USSPACETRAVEL 